// Title: Ping Hex IP
// Sigma ID: 1a0d4aba-7668-4365-9ce4-6d79ab088dfd
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Florian Roth (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2018-03-23 | Modified: 2025-10-17
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1140, T1027
// Description: Detects a ping command that uses a hex encoded IP address
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely, because no sane admin pings IP addresses in a hexadecimal form
// References:
//   - https://github.com/vysecurity/Aggressor-VYSEC/blob/0d61c80387b9432dab64b8b8a9fb52d20cfef80e/ping.cna
//   - https://twitter.com/vysecurity/status/977198418354491392
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_ping_hex_ip.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "\\ping.exe" and ProcessCommandLine matches regex "0x[a-fA-F0-9]{8}"
